    "q-44160": {
      "dom_id": "step-q44160",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14166993/assets/c5659998c906fd3bacea6186206b8670c6449e8700c211844f324a8aae0b84ed.png)\n\nWhat is the slope of the line shown above?",
      "worked_solution": "The line is horizontal, and therefore it has a slope of zero.\n\nWe can see this by choosing two points on the line, such as $(0,4)$ and $(1,4),$ and computing the slope between them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{4-4}{1-0}=\\frac{0}{1}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
